--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 5285559cfa33d7041aa12c3287c4d2a7, type: 3}
  m_Name: 01_THE_BASICS
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::NotebookSection
  number: 01
  title: THE BASICS
  unlocked: 1
  entries:
  - title: 1. Classical bit
    tag: 1
    body: "A <b>bit</b> is the basic unit of classical digital information. At any
      moment it holds exactly one of two values: 0 or 1.\n\n<color=#ffe2a8><b>VALUE  
      THINK OF IT AS   MEANING</b></color>\n  0     Switch OFF       One of the two
      values\n  1     Switch ON        The other value\n\nEverything on an ordinary
      computer, such as text, photos and games, is stored as long strings of bits.
      A classical bit always has one definite value, and you can read it without
      changing it."
    bench: 0
    qiskit: 'from qiskit import ClassicalRegister


      syn = ClassicalRegister(2,
      "syn")   # two classical bits

      out = ClassicalRegister(3, "out")   #
      three more for the data'
  - title: 2. Qubit
    tag: 1
    body: "A <b>qubit</b> (quantum bit) is the basic unit of quantum information.
      When you read it out, you get 0 or 1, just like a bit. The difference is what
      it can be before you read it: its state can be a <b>superposition</b> of the
      two basis states, written |0\u27E9 and |1\u27E9 (see entry 4).\n\nReal qubits
      are physical systems, for example a tiny superconducting circuit, a trapped
      ion or a single photon. They follow the rules of quantum mechanics, which is
      why they behave differently from classical bits.\n\n<color=#ffe2a8><b>                
      CLASSICAL BIT     QUBIT</b></color>\nRead-out values  0 or 1            0 or
      1\nBefore reading   Definitely 0/1    Can be a superposition\nEffect of read  
      None              Updates the qubit's state\nCopy unknown?    Yes, freely      
      No (no-cloning theorem)\n\n<color=#ff8a5c><b>\u258C COMMON MISCONCEPTION</b>\n<indent=0.9em>A
      qubit is not a switch that is secretly 0 or 1 while we simply do not know which.
      Its state follows quantum rules, and measurement probabilities can differ from
      anything a classical bit could produce.</indent></color>"
    bench: 0
    qiskit: "from qiskit import QuantumCircuit\n\nqc = QuantumCircuit(1)   # one
      qubit\n# every qubit starts in |0\u27E9"
  - title: "3. Basis states |0\u27E9 and |1\u27E9"
    tag: 1
    body: "These are the two standard reference states of a qubit. The symbol |\u27E9
      is called a <b>ket</b>. It is the notation physicists use to say \"this is
      a quantum state\", not just a plain number.\n\n\u2022 |0\u27E9: measuring in
      the computational basis returns 0 with certainty.\n\u2022 |1\u27E9: measuring
      in the computational basis returns 1 with certainty.\n\nWhen a qubit is in
      |0\u27E9 or |1\u27E9, it behaves much like a classical bit. That is why this
      puzzle can be solved by reading bit patterns."
    bench: 0
    qiskit: "from qiskit.quantum_info import Statevector\n\nStatevector.from_label(\"0\")  
      # |0\u27E9\nStatevector.from_label(\"1\")   # |1\u27E9"
  - title: 4. Superposition
    tag: 2
    body: "A general single-qubit state mixes the two basis states:\n\n        |\u03C8\u27E9
      = \u03B1|0\u27E9 + \u03B2|1\u27E9\n\nHere \u03B1 and \u03B2 are numbers called
      <b>amplitudes</b> (they can be complex), and together they satisfy |\u03B1|\xB2
      + |\u03B2|\xB2 = 1.\n\nWhen the qubit is measured in the computational basis:\n\u2022
      the probability of getting 0 is |\u03B1|\xB2;\n\u2022 the probability of getting
      1 is |\u03B2|\xB2.\n\n<b>Worked example.</b> If \u03B1 = \u03B2 = 1/\u221A2,
      then |\u03B1|\xB2 = |\u03B2|\xB2 = 1/2. Each measurement gives 0 or 1 with
      a 50% chance, like a fair coin. Run the same circuit many times (each run is
      called a <b>shot</b> in Qiskit) and about half the results will be 0.\n\n<color=#ff8a5c><b>\u258C
      COMMON MISCONCEPTION</b>\n<indent=0.9em>Superposition does not mean the qubit
      shows both 0 and 1 at once. A single measurement gives exactly one outcome.
      It also does not mean \"unknown value\": a superposition is a perfectly definite
      quantum state whose randomness appears only when you measure.</indent></color>"
    bench: 4
    qiskit: "qc = QuantumCircuit(1, 1)\nqc.h(0)            # Hadamard: (|0\u27E9
      + |1\u27E9)/\u221A2\nqc.measure(0, 0)\n# 100 shots -> roughly 50 zeros and
      50 ones"
  - title: 5. Measurement
    tag: 1
    body: "<b>Measurement</b> produces a classical result (0 or 1) from a quantum
      system. For a qubit in superposition, the result is random, with probabilities
      set by the state. Afterwards the qubit is left in the state that matches the
      result you obtained, so a measurement generally changes the qubit.\n\n<color=#3fe0b5><b>\u258C
      IN THIS INVESTIGATION</b>\n<indent=0.9em>The diagnostic console shows results
      from measuring a simulated quantum circuit. Two kinds of measurement matter
      here: the two syndrome bits (which help you find the error) and the final data
      bits (which verify the repair).</indent></color>"
    bench: 4
    qiskit: 'qc.measure(0, 0)    # qubit 0 -> classical bit 0

      qc.measure_all()   
      # or measure every qubit'
  guides:
  - label: Introduction to Qiskit
    url: https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/docs/en/guides/tools-intro
  - label: Plot quantum states
    url: https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/docs/en/guides/plot-quantum-states
  - label: Measure qubits
    url: https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/docs/en/guides/measure-qubits
